"""Authored, self-contained Y10 organic chemistry classes."""
def section(title,paragraphs,example,memory):return dict(title=title,paragraphs=paragraphs,example=example,memory=memory)
def check(prompt,options,answer,why):return dict(prompt=prompt,options=options,answer=answer,why=why)
CLASSES=[
 dict(id='alcohols-carboxylic-acids',title='Alcohols and carboxylic acids',short='Alcohols · Carboxylic acids',intro='Discover why two molecules containing O–H can behave differently. Read their structures, compare ethanol with ethanoic acid and connect the chemistry to fuels and vinegar.',colour='#277f86',objectives=['Read a structural formula and recognise an alcohol or carboxylic acid.','Explain ethanol production and its conversion to ethanoic acid.','Predict a carboxylic acid’s reactions with a base or carbonate.'],sections=[
 section('Start with atoms, bonds and functional groups',[
 'Organic chemistry studies carbon compounds. In these examples carbon, C, forms four covalent bonds; oxygen, O, normally forms two; hydrogen, H, forms one. A covalent bond is a shared pair of electrons. A line represents one bond, and a double line, =, represents two shared pairs.',
 'A structural formula shows which atoms are connected. CH₃–CH₂–OH is a condensed structural formula: the small ₃ and ₂ count hydrogen atoms attached to a carbon. They do not multiply every atom after them. A functional group is a particular arrangement of atoms responsible for characteristic chemical reactions.'
 ],['In CH₃–CH₂–OH, count two carbon atoms, six hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom.','The first carbon has three C–H bonds and one C–C bond; the second has two C–H bonds, one C–C bond and one C–O bond.','The oxygen has one bond to carbon and one to hydrogen: all the usual bond counts fit.'],'Read the connections, not just the letters. A shared group can behave differently when its neighbours change.'),
 section('Alcohols: recognise –OH in the right setting',[
 'The simple alcohols in this class contain a hydroxyl group, –OH, attached to a saturated carbon atom: that carbon has single bonds. This bonded –OH is not a free hydroxide ion, OH⁻. Ethanol is CH₃CH₂OH, also written C₂H₅OH. Methanol, CH₃OH, has one carbon; propan-1-ol, CH₃CH₂CH₂OH, has three.',
 'The ending –ol helps you recognise an alcohol name. The O–H group lets short-chain alcohol molecules interact strongly with water through hydrogen bonding: an attraction involving hydrogen attached to oxygen and a neighbouring oxygen’s electrons. Ethanol mixes with water, while longer carbon chains generally make alcohols less water-soluble. Ethanol is approximately neutral in the usual dilute-water classroom comparison.'
 ],['Read C₂H₅OH as two carbons, five hydrogens in the carbon-containing part and one further hydrogen on oxygen.','The total molecular formula is C₂H₆O.','A molecular formula counts atoms; the structural formula also identifies the alcohol connection.'],'–ol suggests an alcohol; –OH must be read as part of the whole structure.'),
 section('Ethanol in fermentation, fuels and vinegar',[
 'Fermentation uses enzymes in yeast to convert glucose into ethanol and carbon dioxide under oxygen-poor conditions. An enzyme is a biological catalyst: it speeds a reaction without being consumed overall. The simplified equation is C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂. The numbers in front are coefficients, counting relative numbers of molecules.',
 'Industry can also make ethanol by adding water to ethene: C₂H₄ + H₂O ⇌ C₂H₅OH, with suitable catalyst, temperature and pressure. The double arrow means the reaction can proceed in both directions. Ethanol can be used as a fuel or solvent, a liquid that dissolves another substance. Oxidation of ethanol can form ethanoic acid. For this particular conversion, oxidation can be described as gaining oxygen and/or losing hydrogen; it is not a rule that every alcohol gives the same acid.'
 ],['For the simplified overall oxidation, write CH₃CH₂OH + O₂ → CH₃COOH + H₂O.','Count two carbon atoms, six hydrogen atoms and three oxygen atoms on each side.','In vinegar production, microorganisms use oxygen to help convert ethanol into ethanoic acid. Vinegar is a dilute solution, not pure ethanoic acid.'],'Fermentation makes ethanol; oxidation of this primary alcohol can make ethanoic acid.'),
 section('Carboxylic acids: the entire –COOH group matters',[
 'A carboxyl group is –C(=O)–OH, commonly shortened to –COOH. The same carbon is double-bonded to one oxygen and single-bonded to an O–H group. The C=O part is a carbonyl group. This complete arrangement makes a carboxylic acid different from an alcohol, even though both contain O–H.',
 'Ethanoic acid is CH₃COOH. Its name counts both carbon atoms, including the carbon in –COOH. Methanoic acid, HCOOH, has one carbon; propanoic acid, CH₃CH₂COOH, has three. Ethanoic acid is weak in water because only a proportion of its molecules donate a proton, H⁺. In water H⁺ is hydrated; the simplified equation CH₃COOH ⇌ CH₃COO⁻ + H⁺ shows the acid and its ethanoate ion. Weak describes partial ionisation; dilute describes a small amount of acid per solution volume.'
 ],['Compare CH₃CH₂OH and CH₃COOH.','The first has an alcohol –OH; the second has an O–H attached to a carbonyl carbon, giving –COOH.','Do not classify ethanoic acid as an alcohol just because you can see OH in its formula.'],'–COOH is one complete carboxyl group. “Weak” and “dilute” describe different things.'),
 section('Use reactions to distinguish the families',[
 'A carboxylic acid neutralises a suitable base, forming a salt and water. With sodium hydroxide: CH₃COOH + NaOH → CH₃COONa + H₂O. CH₃COONa is sodium ethanoate. Neutralisation means acid and base react; it does not mean any chosen mixture must finish at pH 7. pH measures acidity; pH 7 is neutral for water at about 25 °C.',
 'An acid also reacts with a carbonate or hydrogencarbonate to release carbon dioxide. Effervescence means visible gas bubbles; bubbles alone do not identify the gas. Carbon dioxide turns limewater milky in its standard test. Ethanol does not show the same acid–carbonate effervescence in this ordinary comparison. Its bonded hydroxyl group is not supplying hydroxide ions like sodium hydroxide.'
 ],['For sodium hydrogencarbonate, write CH₃COOH + NaHCO₃ → CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂.','The products are a salt, water and carbon dioxide. NaHCO₃ is the formula of sodium hydrogencarbonate, often called baking soda.','This explains why vinegar and baking soda can produce bubbles. Gas formation is a chemical observation; it is not boiling.'],'Acid + carbonate → salt + water + carbon dioxide. The structure helps explain the observation.'),
 section('Connect the families without mixing them up',[
 'Alcohols and carboxylic acids can react together to make esters. This is the bridge to the next class: alcohol + carboxylic acid ⇌ ester + water under suitable acid-catalysed conditions. A catalyst provides a faster pathway; it is not the alcohol or the acid being converted into the ester.',
 'Use the molecular work mat below to compare ethanol and ethanoic acid. Move the formula into position, select its characteristic group and inspect its behaviour. Record both examples before explaining the difference. The model highlights a structure; it does not simulate the speed or extent of every real reaction.'
 ],['First identify –OH or –COOH from the connections.','Then predict the family and an appropriate reaction: ethanol is an alcohol; ethanoic acid is a weak carboxylic acid.','Use the next class to investigate the ester made from these two molecules.'],'Structure → family → characteristic behaviour. Explain why before memorising a name.')
 ],checks=[
 check('Which structure is an alcohol in this comparison?',['CH₃CH₂OH','CH₃COOH','NaOH'],0,'CH₃CH₂OH has –OH on a saturated carbon. CH₃COOH has a carboxyl group; NaOH is an ionic hydroxide.'),
 check('How many carbon atoms are in ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH?',['One','Two','Three'],1,'Count the CH₃ carbon and the carbon in COOH. The functional-group carbon is part of the chain count.'),
 check('Why is ethanoic acid called weak?',['It must be very dilute.','Only part of it ionises in water.','It contains no hydrogen.'],1,'Strength concerns the extent of ionisation. Concentration concerns the amount per solution volume.'),
 check('What forms in the simplified fermentation of glucose?',['Ethanol and carbon dioxide','Ethanoic acid only','Sodium ethanoate and water'],0,'The simplified equation is C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂.'),
 check('Which gas is expected when ethanoic acid reacts with sodium hydrogencarbonate?',['Hydrogen','Oxygen','Carbon dioxide'],2,'The reaction forms sodium ethanoate, water and CO₂. Gas bubbles are called effervescence.'),
 check('Why is the OH in CH₃COOH not enough to classify it as an alcohol?',['The OH is part of a complete carboxyl group.','There is no oxygen present.','All compounds containing OH are alcohols.'],0,'Read the O–H and C=O together on the same carbon: this is –COOH, the carboxyl group.')]),
 dict(id='esters-polyesters',title='Esters and polyesters',short='Esters · Polyesters',intro='Build an ester from an alcohol and an acid, then discover how two reactive ends turn small molecules into a polyester chain.',colour='#b55f34',objectives=['Identify an ester link and name a simple ester from its two starting compounds.','Explain reversible esterification and the role of a catalyst.','Build a polyester model and count links and water molecules in a finite chain.'],sections=[
 section('A short recap: the two groups that will react',[
 'An alcohol contains an –OH group, and a carboxylic acid contains –C(=O)–OH, shortened to –COOH. A functional group is an atom arrangement responsible for characteristic reactions. C=O is called a carbonyl: the carbon and oxygen share two electron pairs.',
 'In the general formulas R–OH and R–COOH, R stands for a carbon-containing group. R is a placeholder, not the symbol for a chemical element. A second label, R′, means another group that can be different; the prime mark is a label, not a power. Read the entire functional group before predicting a reaction.'
 ],['In ethanol, R–OH can represent CH₃CH₂–OH.','In ethanoic acid, R–COOH can represent CH₃–COOH.','Keep the oxygen in the alcohol and both oxygens of the carboxyl group in view when comparing reactants and products.'],'R means “the rest of the group”; read the functional-group connections explicitly.'),
 section('Esterification: make a new –COO– connection',[
 'Esterification can join a carboxylic acid and an alcohol to form an ester and water. A simple ester contains R–C(=O)–O–R′. The ester link, –COO–, includes a carbonyl next to an oxygen joined to another carbon group. It is not the same as an alcohol –OH or an acid –COOH.',
 'For ethanoic acid and ethanol: CH₃COOH + CH₃CH₂OH ⇌ CH₃COOCH₂CH₃ + H₂O. The ester is ethyl ethanoate. In the school group model, an OH from the acid and an H from the alcohol are shown leaving as water. This tracks the net group change rather than every intermediate in the acid-catalysed mechanism.'
 ],['Count the reactants: two carbons from the acid and two from the alcohol.','The ester has four carbons in total. One water molecule forms in this single ester-link reaction.','Read CH₃COOCH₂CH₃ as CH₃–C(=O)–O–CH₂–CH₃ to see its connectivity.'],'Ester: carbonyl + oxygen bridge, –C(=O)–O–. The bridge connects to another carbon group.'),
 section('Name it, then interpret the reversible arrow',[
 'For the simple esters here, name the alcohol-derived alkyl group first, followed by the acid-derived carboxylate name. Ethanol supplies ethyl; ethanoic acid supplies ethanoate. Methanol and propanoic acid would produce methyl propanoate.',
 'The symbol ⇌ means the reaction can proceed forwards and backwards. At equilibrium, the two reaction rates are equal and composition stays constant; the amounts of reactants and products do not have to be equal. An acid catalyst speeds the approach to equilibrium in both directions. A catalyst alone does not change the equilibrium mixture at a fixed temperature.'
 ],['Identify the alcohol before choosing the first name: ethanol → ethyl.','Identify the acid for the second name: ethanoic acid → ethanoate.','Combine them as ethyl ethanoate. Do not swap the two origins when naming the ester.'],'Alcohol first, acid second: ethyl + ethanoate. ⇌ means both directions, not equal amounts.'),
 section('From a small ester to a long polyester',[
 'A monomer is a small molecule that can join to others. A polymer is a large molecule containing many linked units. A diol has two alcohol groups, one at each end; a dicarboxylic acid has two carboxyl groups. “Di-” means two. These complementary ends let a chain keep growing.',
 'For example, HO–CH₂–CH₂–OH is a diol. Each –OH can form an ester link with an acid group. A dicarboxylic acid such as HOOC–CH₂–CH₂–COOH can link at both its ends too. The resulting chain has repeated –COO– ester links and is a polyester. Ordinary ethanol and ethanoic acid each have only one suitable reactive group, so their esterification does not produce a long chain.'
 ],['Lay out one diol and one dicarboxylic acid.','Join an alcohol end to an acid end and remove the net H + OH as H₂O.','Two reactive ends remain available to extend the chain with more bifunctional monomers.'],'Two ends let the chain extend. A polyester is recognised by repeated ester links.'),
 section('Count water correctly in a finite chain',[
 'Condensation polymerisation joins suitable monomers while releasing a small molecule. For the diol + dicarboxylic acid route shown here, each new ester link releases one water molecule. Other condensation routes can release different small molecules; always check the actual reactants.',
 'A single, unbranched, open chain made from N starting monomer molecules has N − 1 links. N is the number of molecules joined, not their number of atoms. With n diols and n diacids, N = 2n, so the finite chain has 2n − 1 links and releases 2n − 1 waters. This model excludes rings and branches and leaves two end groups. In a textbook repeat unit, the subscript n usually counts repeats; read what n means in each equation.'
 ],['Choose three pairs: three diols and three diacids make six starting molecules.','Join six molecules into one open chain: 6 − 1 = 5 links.','Five links release five waters in this model. A repeat-unit drawing can omit the two end groups; the finite chain calculation cannot.'],'One open chain: links = starting molecules − 1. One water per ester link in this chosen route.'),
 section('Polyesters in everyday materials and recycling',[
 'Many small esters have characteristic odours and are used in formulated fragrances or flavourings. An odour is not a reliable identification test, and being an ester does not make a chemical safe to taste or smell. Fats and oils also contain ester connections.',
 'PET, poly(ethylene terephthalate), is a polyester used in bottles and fibres. Its repeated ester connections can be broken by hydrolysis, a reaction with water, under suitable chemical conditions. Hydrolysis is not simply melting: it breaks chemical bonds. Not every polyester biodegrades quickly, and an ester link is not a guarantee that a plastic can be composted in ordinary conditions. Use the bench to compare one small ester with a finite polyester chain.'
 ],['Look for repeated –COO– links when classifying the chain.','In the model, select Ester to make one link, then Polyester and vary the number of monomer pairs.','Compare the water count with the number of new links, not with the number of carbon atoms.'],'Polyester says which links repeat; it does not promise instant biodegradation.')
 ],checks=[
 check('Which atom arrangement identifies the ester link?',['–C(=O)–O–','–C(=O)–NH–','C=C only'],0,'An ester contains a carbonyl next to the bridging oxygen. An amide contains nitrogen at that position.'),
 check('Ethanol and ethanoic acid form which ester?',['Ethanoic ethanol','Ethyl ethanoate','Methyl propanoate'],1,'Ethanol supplies ethyl and ethanoic acid supplies ethanoate.'),
 check('What does an acid catalyst do at fixed temperature?',['Guarantees 100% ester.','Speeds both reaction directions without changing the equilibrium mixture.','Becomes the polymer backbone.'],1,'A catalyst speeds the approach to equilibrium; it does not itself change the equilibrium composition.'),
 check('Which pair can grow into a polyester by this route?',['A diol and a dicarboxylic acid','Ethanol and ethanoic acid alone','Only ethene molecules'],0,'Each monomer needs two complementary reactive ends. Ethene instead forms an addition polymer.'),
 check('Three diols and three diacids form one finite open chain. How many waters are released?',['Six','Three','Five'],2,'Six monomer molecules form five links. Each ester link releases one H₂O in this route.'),
 check('Does every polyester rapidly biodegrade in ordinary conditions?',['Yes, because it contains oxygen.','No; its structure and the conditions matter.','Yes, because melting is hydrolysis.'],1,'Hydrolysis breaks bonds under suitable conditions. Melting is a physical change, and an ester link alone does not guarantee compostability.')]),
 dict(id='amines-amides-polyamides',title='Amines, amides and polyamides',short='Amines · Amides · Polyamides',intro='Follow nitrogen’s electrons to distinguish an amine from an amide, then build the amide links found in nylon and proteins.',colour='#6d58a5',objectives=['Distinguish amines from amides by reading nitrogen’s neighbours.','Explain why a simple amine can accept H⁺ and why an amide is much less basic.','Identify suitable polyamide monomers and count links in a finite chain.'],sections=[
 section('Meet nitrogen and its lone pair',[
 'Nitrogen, N, is the new atom in this class. A simple neutral nitrogen centre commonly has three covalent bonds and one lone pair: two outer-shell electrons not used in those bonds. Covalent bonds are shared electron pairs. A lone pair can sometimes form a bond to an electron-pair acceptor.',
 'In a primary amine such as methylamine, CH₃NH₂, the nitrogen is attached to a carbon group and two hydrogens. Other amines can have more carbon groups on nitrogen, so –NH₂ is not the only possible amine pattern. Here we begin with primary amines and compare the atoms directly next to nitrogen.'
 ],['Read CH₃–NH₂: carbon is attached to nitrogen, and nitrogen to two hydrogens.','The nitrogen has three bonds in total and a lone pair.','There is no carbonyl, C=O, on the carbon directly bonded to nitrogen in this simple amine.'],'Find N, then check its neighbours. Not every nitrogen-containing compound is an amine.'),
 section('Why a simple amine can act as a base',[
 'A Brønsted–Lowry base is a proton acceptor. A proton in these acid–base equations is H⁺. The available lone pair on methylamine can form a bond to H⁺: CH₃NH₂ + H⁺ → CH₃NH₃⁺. The product is the methylammonium ion. Its positive charge belongs to the whole ion.',
 'In water, only a proportion of simple amine molecules accept a proton from water: CH₃NH₂ + H₂O ⇌ CH₃NH₃⁺ + OH⁻. This gives a weakly basic solution. Weak describes the extent of reaction in water, not how concentrated the solution is. With hydrochloric acid, the resulting ions can form a methylammonium chloride salt.'
 ],['Start with methylamine, CH₃NH₂.','Add H⁺ to nitrogen using its lone pair. Nitrogen now has four bonds.','Write CH₃NH₃⁺, including the charge. The molecule has accepted a proton; it has not lost its carbon group.'],'Amine: an available lone pair can welcome H⁺. Remember the new positive charge.'),
 section('Amides: put the carbonyl next to nitrogen',[
 'An amide contains a nitrogen directly bonded to a carbonyl carbon: –C(=O)–N–. A primary example is ethanamide, CH₃CONH₂, more explicitly CH₃–C(=O)–NH₂. The carbon in the carbonyl is included in the name’s carbon count, so ethanamide has two carbons.',
 'In an amide, nitrogen’s lone pair interacts with the neighbouring carbonyl system. Delocalised means the electrons are spread across more than one atom rather than confined to one local pair. The pair is therefore less available to accept H⁺. Amides are much less basic than comparable amines. The explanation depends on connectivity; both molecules can contain an NH₂ group yet belong to different families.'
 ],['Compare CH₃–NH₂ with CH₃–C(=O)–NH₂.','In the second structure, nitrogen touches the carbonyl carbon directly.','Classify the first as methylamine and the second as ethanamide, then connect the carbonyl interaction to the reduced basicity.'],'Amide = amine-like N beside a carbonyl. The neighbouring C=O changes the electron behaviour.'),
 section('Form an amide link under appropriate conditions',[
 'A carboxylic acid contains –COOH. An amine group and an acid group can be converted into an amide link under suitable condensation conditions, with net water loss: –COOH + H₂N– → –CONH– + H₂O. The link contains C(=O)–N; it is not an ester’s C(=O)–O connection.',
 'Simply mixing an ordinary carboxylic acid and amine at room temperature often gives an ammonium carboxylate salt through an acid–base reaction: the acid transfers H⁺ to the amine, producing oppositely charged ammonium and carboxylate ions. Forming the amide requires suitable activation, dehydration or heating conditions. Our draggable group model represents the net bond-forming change under appropriate conditions; it does not claim that dropping any two room-temperature solutions together instantly makes nylon.'
 ],['Identify the acid –COOH and the amine –NH₂ end.','In the net group model, remove OH from the acid and H from the amine as H₂O.','Join the carbonyl carbon to nitrogen: –C(=O)–NH–. Read this as a bond map, not a full reaction mechanism.'],'Salt formation and amide formation are different processes. Conditions matter.'),
 section('Two-ended monomers can build a polyamide',[
 'A diamine has two amine groups, and a dicarboxylic acid has two carboxyl groups. “Di-” means two. A monomer is a small starting molecule; a polymer is a much larger chain built by linking many units. Complementary reactive ends allow repeated amide-link formation, producing a polyamide.',
 'In the acid + diamine route shown, each new amide link releases water. A single finite, unbranched, open chain made from N starting molecules has N − 1 links. N counts monomer molecules, not atoms. For n diamines and n diacids, there are 2n starting molecules, 2n − 1 links and 2n − 1 waters, leaving two terminal groups. This count excludes rings and branches; repeat-unit notation commonly omits end groups.'
 ],['Use two diamines and two diacids: four starting molecules.','Make one open chain with 4 − 1 = 3 new amide links.','Three waters form in this chosen acid + amine route. Changing to five pairs means ten starting molecules and nine waters.'],'Two ends allow growth. In one open chain, links = monomer molecules − 1.'),
 section('From nylon ropes to peptide links in proteins',[
 'Nylon-6,6 is a polyamide made using a six-carbon diamine and a six-carbon diacid. For the diacid, remember to count the two carboxyl carbons. Attractions between suitable amide groups on neighbouring chains, including hydrogen bonds (attractions involving H attached to N or O and a nearby N or O lone pair), contribute to useful fibre properties. Chain structure and processing also influence strength; no single bond type explains every material property.',
 'Proteins contain amide connections called peptide bonds between amino-acid residues. An amino acid has both an amine-containing group and a carboxyl group, so its connectivity differs from a separate diamine + diacid pair. Cells form proteins through controlled biochemical machinery; dragging groups in a model illustrates the linkage rather than reproducing the full cellular process. Compare nitrogen’s neighbours first, then use the polyamide bench to build and inspect a link.'
 ],['An ester chain repeats –COO–; a polyamide chain repeats –CONH– in these examples.','Both are condensation polymers for the routes taught here, but the atom next to the carbonyl differs: O or N.','Use this distinction to identify a nylon link and a peptide link without calling every nitrogen compound an amine.'],'Ester bridge: O. Amide bridge: N. The whole chain keeps repeating the characteristic link.')
 ],checks=[
 check('Which molecule is a simple amine?',['CH₃NH₂','CH₃CONH₂','CH₃COOH'],0,'Methylamine has nitrogen bonded to a carbon group without an adjacent carbonyl carbon.'),
 check('What does methylamine form when it accepts H⁺?',['CH₃NH₃⁺','CH₃NH⁻','CH₃COOH'],0,'The nitrogen lone pair forms a bond to H⁺. The methylammonium product has four N bonds and a positive charge.'),
 check('Why is ethanamide much less basic than a comparable amine?',['It contains no nitrogen.','Its nitrogen lone pair interacts with the neighbouring carbonyl.','Every nitrogen lone pair is equally available.'],1,'Delocalisation into the carbonyl system reduces the availability of the nitrogen lone pair for proton acceptance.'),
 check('Does simply mixing an ordinary amine and carboxylic acid at room temperature guarantee an amide?',['Yes, instantly.','No; an acid–base salt can form, and amide formation needs suitable conditions.','No, because amides contain no carbon.'],1,'Distinguish ammonium carboxylate salt formation from the bond-forming condensation under appropriate conditions.'),
 check('Which pair forms a polyamide in the chosen model?',['A diamine and a dicarboxylic acid','A diol and a dicarboxylic acid','Ethene and water'],0,'Two amine ends and two acid ends support repeated –CONH– links. A diol + diacid makes a polyester.'),
 check('Five diamines and five diacids form one finite open chain. How many waters form?',['Ten','Five','Nine'],2,'Ten starting molecules need nine links to make one open chain. Each link releases one H₂O in this route.')])
]
